			<div class="content" id="post-content-4861610">
									

<p>Hello Angryman22! let's take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: A gun store named ‘Gun-thers Armory’. It has all the amenities you would expect (Firing Room, Break Room, etc).It is open at almost all hours, closing at 23:30 on Saturday, and reopening at 0:00 on Sunday. Every time the store is reopened, there will be 1 firearm inside a glass case that is anomalous. The glass case will not restock until the store closes and reopens. It also has some other minor anomalous properties, but they seem to minor to list.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall, the concept (to me) is lacking in context and purpose. There doesn't seem to be any clear reason why the store is here, and why there is always one gun that is anomalous. Currently there's nothing that really gets me invested, as the store doesn't really have anything behind the initial effects (which are rather mild on their own). -why is the gun always behind a glass case? what makes said gun anomalous? how does this aspect impact the narrative? what makes the specific details listed in the pitch important?</p>
<p>Along with this, even minor effects should at least be mentioned. As the ones mentioned here do seem to play apart in the narrative, I would recommend you give context to what exactly they are. And how they relate to the core idea.</p>
<p>Quick note; &quot;closing at 23:30 on Saturday, and reopening at 0:00 on Sunday&quot;, it feels oddly specific that the store is only closed for a maximum of half an hour a week. Considering this effect doesn't really seem to bare any impact on the anomaly.- what impact does this have on the narrative and concept?</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The foundation investigates the store under the guise of “Safety Inspectors”.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't really enough of a setup, as it doesn't really give context as to how the skip was initially found. How did the Foundation initially find the store? considering the anomalies aren't immediately obvious, how did this place initially get the Foundations attention?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They investigate the place’s 1 worker who works there for the entire time (anomalously severe case of insomnia lets him work all week.).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This element feels unnecessary, considering the 'insomnia' he deals with doesn't seem to play any major (or minor) role in the narrative or concept (nor is it really explained). As it stands, this feels unrelated to everything and like a quick way to explain why one person can constantly work at the shop.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The guy believes all of the weaponry is non-anomalous and considers it as irrelevant to talk about his job, so the foundation just puts a mic on their glasses to monitor them.As for the store itself, the foundation try to make the minor anomalies seem normal to the public, and just buy the anomalous gun the second the store opens.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is the character this way? what makes him think everything is entirely normal? and why isn't he interested in talking about his job?- he was hired to sell guns, it seems odd that he doesn't want to talk about that.</p>
<p>Along with this, why is the Foundation so set on leaving this open to the public? it seems much more plausible for them to take the employee in for questioning (and close the store), rather than trying to covertly watch the store while it runs as usual.- Whats their reason behind this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Other than that, it’s just testing the weaponry out in test logs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Logs and tests alone won't really be able to keep audience attention, as they can't show the entire narrative arc without feeling repetitive, or becoming a narrative device. Along with this, the skip and its effects alone won't be able to carry the narrative, as there isn't much to really get care about or look into beyond what the skip initially does.- what about the guns anomies will make as the reader invested?</p>
<p>Overall, the narrative lacks a strong conflict and arc, which causes the story to feel directionless and a bit anti-climatic, as there isn't really an end goal, motive, purpose or conflict shown here. Currently, the narrative only really feels like a couple of events that don't have much of a link between them.- There also isn't any details or mentions of what exactly is tested.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook/Attention-Grabber: MTFs are allowed to use any weapons created, excluding those deemed unsafe for usage by the ethics committee, those ineffective for what the foundation requires from their weaponry, or those who have too minor an effect to replace standard issue weaponry.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels closer to additional info than a hook, as this wasn't really depicted in the narrative and doesn't really offer a clear direction (narrative wise) or show the core idea.- how would you show this in the narrative?</p>
<p>That said, I would recommend you show this more in narrative. As it gives the story a clearer direction, and allows for you to expand on the concept further.</p>
<p><strong>Here are some general questions that may also help with narrative development</strong><br />
What are the Foundations motives? -why do they choose to allow the store to operate to the public? what is the skips main purpose?- how will that purpose be communicated in the narrative? who are the characters?- what role will they play in everything? how will that make me as the reader feel? what is the narrative building up to?- how will everything end? what is the main theme?</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques/perspectives in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
