			<div class="content" id="post-content-4862419">
									

<p>Heyo follow up!</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The store itself is now owned by a parent company (potentially MC&amp;D). The reason there is only 1 gun a week is to cause customers to keep coming back to the store and potentially spend more money than they originally anticipated.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I like the MC&amp;D Addition, though, this element doesn't really seem to be brought up again? at least not in much detail. As a result, the GOI feels like a quick placeholder to explain why the store continuously runs and operates.-what is their motive behind this?</p>
<p>-That said, I'm not too familiar MC&amp;D as whole.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Basically, its just a greedy company. Its behind a glass case because they want people's eyes to be drawn to it an make it look valuable. The gun is made anomalous by unknown means which the foundation will try to investigate. Its only closed for a half hour because the greedy company wants to make a lot of money. As for the minor anomalies, its mostly quality of life stuff for the employee and the building, like a restocking fridge in the break room, and an indestructible firing gallery.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My biggest question/issue is motive behind it, while it is clear they want to maximise profits, there isn't much showing <em>why</em> they are so set on this endeavour in particular. And why they are so set on investing so much into a relatively small business.- for the amount of profit they would make, it seems odd that they are so set on keeping this one place open.</p>
<p>Another issue I have is the weapons 'unknown means'. As it stands, there isn't much showing what came out of this investigation, which makes the narrative unfinished and a bit anti-climatic, as there's no clear answer shown here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The foundation found the place after investigating a surge in anomalous weapons in the local area. The 1 employee thing is again meant to show the greedy company wanting to make as little spending as they can, but I can change the 1 employee if you feel it would be better. The person no longer thinks the guns are non-anomalous, but the dont talk about their job outside of work because they feel it would be very boring to do so because to them it feels like an accountant talking about how they filed their drawer and such, you understand?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Considering this employee gets only 30mins off per week, I'm skeptical as to how he/she would manage to talk about their job with others in that period of time, if it wasn't planed. Along with this, I have trouble believing he/she is here willingly? why would someone give their life up for a job they don't consider important enough to talk about?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The foundation now actively tries to shut down the building through several means, but they keep getting reverted because of the company's seemingly endless supply of funds. They do eventually shut it down by just demolishing it and relocating the shooting gallery.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This aspect makes me worried, as the endless funds make the &quot;company/GOI&quot; feel far to OP.- It pretty much feels like a quick way to allow the store to do anything with no consequences or setbacks.</p>
<p>Along with this, I don't believe the Foundation would try to continuously re-buy or shut down the building itself, if they know it won't work.</p>
<p>(to me) it seems more plausible for them to buy the town or a large sum of land around the building, and close it off to the public that way.- killing it seems un-characteristically destructive of the Foundation, they would only really attempt to kill something if it was absolutely necessary.</p>
<hr />
<p>In general, the narrative hasn't really changed to much.- It more or less feels like some explanations were sprinkled in here and there, but there's no direction to the narrative or much of a conflict present.- most of my previous questions and issues with the narrative still stand.</p>
<p>In general I'd recommend looking more into the GOI as whole, it may give some insight into how you could tell the narrative or give some interesting potential directions/hooks.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques/perspectives in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

